  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY NIGHT THROUGH MONDAY/...\nIssued at 201 PM MDT Tue Jul 28 2026\n\nKey Messages...\n\n-Expect scattered showers and thunderstorms for Thursday and Friday, \nwith drier and hotter weather over the weekend. Temperatures will \nnot be as hot for Monday and Tuesday.\n\nThursday...Northwest flow aloft develops in the wake of a passing \nshortwave trough aloft, with drying noted across southeast Wyoming \nand monsoonal moisture moves into Nebraska, we will see isolated to \nscattered late day showers and thunderstorms across the Nebraska \nPanhandle.\n\nFriday...Monsoonal moisture plume stretches north to south across \nfar eastern Wyoming and western Nebraska, and with a ridge runner \nshortwave trough aloft in northwest flow, we expect to see scattered \nshowers and thunderstorms generally east of a Douglas to Cheyenne \nline.\n\nSaturday and Sunday...Ridging prevails with a decrease in monsoonal \nmoisture and warm temperatures aloft will produce enough CIN, \nConvective Inhibition, to prevent thunderstorm development. Hot \ntemperatures return with the hottest day being Sunday, and with 700 \nmb temperatures from 20 to 23 Celsius, high temperatures will range \nfrom 95 to 105 degrees, with possible Heat Advisories needed east of \nI-25 on Sunday.\n\nMonday and Tuesday...Broad troughing aloft develops across the \nnorthern Rockies with zonal flow over our counties. This will help \nproduce somewhat cooler temperatures as 700 mb temperatures lower to \n13 Celsius. There may be enough low and mid level moisture to \nproduce isolated late day thunderstorms over and near the Laramie \nValley and southern Laramie Range.",
